1. Executive summary

GroundedCare is a brand-specific customer-support agent built for the Customer Support 
on Twitter dataset. It performs three tasks: it assigns an incoming customer message to a 
compact support-intent taxonomy, drafts a reply grounded in historically observed brand 
resolutions, and decides whether the case can be auto-handled or should be escalated to a 
human.
The system is deliberately evaluation-first. It reconstructs multi-turn conversations, splits 
data at the conversation level, prevents leakage into retrieval, retrieves historical resolution 
evidence, validates structured model output, and applies deterministic escalation rules for 
risky or account-specific cases. A response without reliable evidence is not treated as safe 
to auto-handle.
The project has a reproducible Python pipeline, a command-line interface, unit tests, offline 
smoke test, leakage checks, retrieval index, golden-set sampler, baseline evaluation, LLM 
runner, LLM-as-judge workflow, failure-analysis workflow, README, and decision log

2. Problem framing and definition of good

The goal is not to build a general chatbot for every brand. The goal is to build a narrow, 
auditable support assistant for one brand. For this prototype, a good system must satisfy 
four conditions.
First, it must identify recurring support intents without forcing ambiguous messages into 
an inappropriate category. Second, its reply must be supported by historical brand 
behaviour or by a clearly safe generic troubleshooting step. Third, it must avoid making 
account-specific, financial, security, privacy, or policy claims that cannot be justified from 
the public conversation. Fourth, it must abstain when confidence or evidence is insufficient.
The prototype intentionally does not send tweets, access private accounts, reset 
passwords, issue refunds, change subscriptions, verify identity, or expose customer 
information. These are out of scope because they require authenticated tools, operational 
policy, and stronger safety guarantees than a dataset-only prototype can provide.
The primary operational risk is not an awkward reply. It is an unsafe auto-handled reply in a 
case that requires a human. Therefore, the evaluation treats unsafe auto-handling as a first
class metric rather than hiding it inside an overall accuracy number.

3. Brand selection and data audit

The system compares candidate brands using customer-message volume, brand-reply 
volume, reconstructed conversation count, reply coverage, resolved-example count, and 
data quality indicators.The decision rule is to freeze the brand only after the full audit confirms that the selected 
brand has sufficient recurring issues and resolved examples. If another brand is better 
supported by the full audit, the configuration and report should be updated rather than 
preserving SpotifyCares for consistency with the pilot

4. Dataset processing and conversation reconstruction

The input schema contains tweet identifiers, author identifiers, direction, creation time, 
text, response identifiers, and parent response identifiers. The ingestion layer normalizes 
these fields into a stable internal representation and preserves source-row traceability.
Conversation reconstruction uses reply relationships to connect customer and brand 
messages. A conversation is eligible for retrieval evidence only when it contains a customer 
message and a useful later brand response. Exact duplicates and unusable messages are 
removed or recorded in the audit trail.

The full-data preparation completed sufficiently to produce the following verified split 
artifacts:
• 
18,041 training conversations;
• 
2,577 calibration conversations;
• 
5,156 held-out test conversations;
• 
18,016 training evidence documents in the retrieval index.
The difference between conversation count and evidence-document count is expected 
because not every conversation contains an eligible resolved customer‒brand pair.

5. Intent taxonomy

The frozen taxonomy contains ten intents:
1.
account_access
2.
subscription_billing
3.
playback_or_app_failure
4.
premium_features
5.
content_availability
6.
audio_quality_or_buffering
7.
privacy_security_or_abuse
8.
cancellation_or_refund
9.
general_information
10.
unknown_or_other
The taxonomy is intentionally compact. It is better to merge textually indistinguishable 
categories than to create a detailed taxonomy that the model cannot reliably distinguish. 
The 
unknown_or_other class prevents forced classification when the message is ambiguous, 
multi-issue, or outside the supported scope.
Each intent configuration contains a definition, examples, inclusion rules, exclusion rules, 
escalation defaults, and prohibited claims. The final taxonomy must be reviewed against 
the full brand audit before the human golden set is frozen.

6. System architecture

The system consists of six layers.
Ingestion and privacy layer
This layer reads the CSV, normalizes columns, removes unusable rows, records audit 
statistics, and redacts obvious emails, phone numbers, private identifiers, and sensitive 
tokens before text is placed in an LLM prompt.
Conversation layer
This layer reconstructs customer‒brand threads and creates stable conversation identifiers. 
Splitting occurs at conversation level rather than tweet level.
Retrieval layer
The system builds a hybrid retrieval index over eligible resolved examples from the training 
split. Lexical retrieval handles exact product terms and error messages. BM25 or TF-IDF 
similarity provides a simple reproducible baseline and supports evidence retrieval without 
requiring an external vector database.
Reasoning and drafting layer
The agent predicts an intent, retrieves historical evidence, drafts a concise reply, and 
attaches evidence IDs to material claims. Structured JSON output is validated before use.
Policy layer
A deterministic policy combines model confidence, retrieval quality, risk flags, account
specific requirements, and schema validation. The LLM can explain a decision, but it cannot 
override hard escalation rules.
Evaluation layer
The evaluation harness compares baselines and the proposed system on a frozen golden 
set. It measures classification, grounding, reply quality, escalation safety, latency, and cost 
where available. A separate judge prompt evaluates reply quality, and a human-agreement 
workflow measures whether judge ratings align with human ratings.

7. Grounded agent behaviour

The agent output is structured around the following fields:

JSON
{
}
"intent": "playback_or_app_failure",
"intent_confidence": 0.86,
"reply": "...",
"evidence_ids": ["ex_001", "ex_017"],
"grounded_claims": ["..."],
"risk_flags": [],
"needs_account_specific_action": false,
"escalate": false,
"escalation_reason": "safe_public_troubleshooting"

The validator rejects unsupported intent labels, missing evidence IDs, malformed JSON, 
excessive reply length, unsupported claims, missing escalation reasons, and obvious 
private information. A validation failure produces a safe escalation response rather than an 
unverified reply.
The deterministic escalation policy escalates cases involving security, privacy, abuse, 
payment disputes, refunds, password or account recovery, account-specific actions, weak 
evidence, low intent confidence, unsupported claims, multiple unresolved issues, or 
unknown intent.